    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! Traditionalist theologians typically approach recent changes in marriage and family definitions from a perspective grounded in longstanding doctrinal beliefs, historical interpretations, and a strong reliance on sacred texts.\n\nAt the moral heart of their arguments, they often emphasize the sanctity and predefined structure of family and marriage as they understand them, primarily using Genesis 1:27 (\"God created man in his own image... male and female he created them.\") and Matthew 19:4-6 (\"There was a man, who claimed to be Abraham's son, and he received him in... to give him his daughter in marriage, but the son-in-law refused and said, 'I do not think that I can look on anyone as lustful. If I take her I would be committing adultery.' So he refused\") as scriptural backing for their views.\n\nThey are concerned that redefining marriage to include same-sex unions and non-traditional family structures undermines the traditional male-female partnership foundational to their moral and social teachings. Traditionalist theologians argue that these changes threaten the institution of marriage and thereby compromise the ethical upbringing of children, the historical and cultural legacy, and the spiritual principles intended by divine mandate.\n\nThe core moral reasoning is deeply rooted in the belief that",
